# Golden vectors for the PNG worked example.
#
# The signature is not transcribed from RFC 2083; it is the first eight bytes
# of a PNG some other encoder wrote -- `/usr/share/icons/cab_extract.png`,
# which `file(1)` reports as "PNG image data, 22 x 22, 8-bit/color RGBA,
# non-interlaced". Reading it off a real file rather than off the RFC is the
# point: a description that agrees only with the document it was written from
# has not been held to anything that ships.
#
# It agrees with RFC 2083 section 3.1, which is the check rather than the
# source -- the RFC gives the same eight bytes and explains why each was
# chosen: the high bit catches a seven-bit transport, `PNG` names the format
# in a hex dump, the CRLF-LF pair catches a line-ending translation, and the
# ^Z stops a DOS `type` from printing the rest.
#
# WHY ONLY THIS STRUCT. `situc build --owned` emits a fixed-size decode, and
# `png_signature` is the one struct here with a fixed size -- a `chunk` is
# `length` bytes of data and a whole file is a run of them. So this corpus
# exercises what `--owned` owns and no more, which is what the round-trip
# test asks of it. The chunk machinery is exercised by the generated checks
# and by the four-backend differential instead.

png_signature signature 89 50 4E 47 0D 0A 1A 0A
